```c
// naive: for large N, B's column access thrashes cache every step
for (i = 0; i < N; i++)
  for (j = 0; j < N; j++)
    for (k = 0; k < N; k++)
      C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j];

// blocked: process BxB tiles that fit in cache before moving on
for (ii = 0; ii < N; ii += B)
  for (jj = 0; jj < N; jj += B)
    for (kk = 0; kk < N; kk += B)
      for (i = ii; i < ii+B; i++)
        for (j = jj; j < jj+B; j++)
          for (k = kk; k < kk+B; k++)
            C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j];
```

Same arithmetic, same result — but the blocked version reuses each tile of A, B, and C many times while it's still in cache, instead of streaming through memory-sized rows and columns on every iteration.

```c
struct Counters {
  long thread0_count;  // adjacent -> same 64-byte cache line
  long thread1_count;
};

// Thread 0 repeatedly increments thread0_count
// Thread 1 repeatedly increments thread1_count
// Despite touching different fields, every write on either
// thread invalidates the whole line for the other thread.
```

The fix: pad each counter to its own cache line (e.g. `long thread0_count; char pad[56];`), so the two threads' writes no longer collide on shared hardware even though the variables were always logically independent.
